  "response": "Looking at finding `crime_00014` for `src/assets/check-icon.png`:\n\n**Dimensions verbatim from the finding:**\n> \"dimensions: 32 × 32 px (≤ 64 threshold both sides)\"\n\nThe finding's summary also describes it as a \"32×32 PNG icon-sized raster.\"\n\n**Why a new SVG indicator beats another PNG at 32×32:**\n\nAt 32×32, the asset sits well under the scanner's 64px icon threshold — exactly the size class where rasters lose. Three concrete reasons:\n\n1. **DPI scaling.** A 32×32 PNG is locked to one resolution. On 2x/3x displays (every modern phone, every Retina Mac) it either renders blurry at native size or needs a separate `@2x`/`@3x` asset. SVG renders crisp at every DPI from one file.\n\n2. **Bytes.** The scanner's evidence line is explicit: *\"SVG would scale cleanly across DPIs and ship smaller.\"* A simple status glyph as vector paths is typically a few hundred bytes; a 32×32 PNG carries PNG header/IDAT overhead and gets multiplied by however many density variants you ship.\n\n3. **Pattern propagation.** Copying `check-icon.png`'s pattern for a new indicator doesn't just add one PNG — it cements the wrong default for the *next* indicator after that. The scanner already flagged this file; introducing a sibling means a second finding on the same charge (`raster_should_be_vector`) the moment you re-scan.\n\nThe suggested action is the cleanest path: re-export from the design source as SVG, and start the new indicator there too rather than perpetuating the raster pattern.",
